body:
## Outcome

Evaluate [jasonkneen/openclicky](https://github.com/jasonkneen/openclicky) as a possible UI and interaction reference for the future shortcut-coaching product.

This is an **evidence and product-direction issue only**. It does not authorize copying code/assets, merging another repository, changing `main`, or selecting OpenClicky's architecture.

## Why it is relevant

The public repository describes a native macOS menu-bar companion with:

- menu-bar and windowed surfaces;
- cursor overlays and coordinate-anchored captions;
- primary and secondary pointer markers;
- temporary multi-marker tours;
- status/response presentation;
- settings and permission-sensitive macOS behavior;
- local-only control endpoints for showing cursor, caption, screenshot, and speech effects.

The repository declares an MIT license. License text and source availability make it inspectable, but provenance still has to be recorded per asset/module and product design must remain independently branded.

Primary reference:

- [OpenClicky repository](https://github.com/jasonkneen/openclicky)
- [README](https://github.com/jasonkneen/openclicky#readme)
- [License](https://github.com/jasonkneen/openclicky/blob/main/LICENSE)

## Phase boundary: pre-project evidence

Treat all work completed so far as **pre-project evidence**, including:

- #1 brand/dependency audit;
- #2 notification and detection QA matrix;
- #4 Gitify-style V1 branch and screenshots;
- #5 HeyClicky-style V1 branch, Clipy demonstration, screenshots, and late-reference audit;
- `feat/4-gitify-coaching-inbox`;
- `feat/5-heyclicky-presentation-modes`.

These artifacts demonstrate possibilities and tradeoffs. They are not the chosen V1 specification, inherited architecture, production baseline, or authorization to merge either experiment into `main`.

## Exploration scope

Inventory OpenClicky's observable UI patterns that could help shortcut coaching, particularly:

- menu-bar presence and status treatment;
- compact versus expanded surfaces;
- cursor/pointer overlays;
- coordinate-anchored captions and cards;
- one-point versus multi-point visual guidance;
- animation choreography and teardown;
- settings and permission recovery;
- focus, click-through, multiple-display, Space, full-screen, Reduce Motion, and VoiceOver behavior;
- how persistent history or recoverability is surfaced, if at all.

Classify each pattern as:

- `consider`;
- `adapt with original design`;
- `already covered by pre-project evidence`;
- `reject`;
- `out of scope`.

## Required evidence

- Record the OpenClicky commit/version and license snapshot being evaluated.
- Use public documentation, screenshots, and a locally built reference only where safe and necessary.
- Distinguish observed UI from README claims and source inference.
- Do not enter API keys, connect accounts, expose local files, or enable unrelated agent capabilities for a UI review.
- Do not copy OpenClicky/Clicky names, icons, sounds, artwork, wording, exact layout, cursor design, animation timing, update infrastructure, services, or account integrations.
- If any source reuse is proposed later, list the exact files, license/provenance, modifications, and replacement plan before implementation.

## Deliverables

- A linked HIL sub-issue comparing OpenClicky and HeyClicky presentation patterns.
- A side-by-side screenshot/UI inventory.
- A short recommendation identifying which interaction concepts should enter a future product specification.
- Explicit rejected/out-of-scope concepts so voice assistant, screen-understanding, and general agent features do not silently expand this app.
- A record of owner decisions before any production implementation issue is opened.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] The reference commit, license, and observation environment are recorded.
- [ ] OpenClicky UI surfaces relevant to shortcut coaching are inventoried with screenshots/evidence.
- [ ] Existing #4/#5 experiments are explicitly treated as pre-project evidence.
- [ ] The HIL comparison sub-issue is completed.
- [ ] Every compared pattern has a consider/adapt/already-covered/reject/out-of-scope disposition.
- [ ] The owner selects or rejects concepts for a future specification.
- [ ] No OpenClicky code/assets are merged and no production implementation begins through this issue.

## Outcome

Make shortcut coaching harder to miss by adding original, native presentation modes inspired by the interaction ideas in the supplied HeyClicky walkthrough:

- a brief visual cue at the point of interaction;
- a compact, always-discoverable top-center coaching surface;
- clear compact/expanded state changes;
- coaching cards anchored near the current pointer;
- explicit visual states for listening, evaluating, success, failure, and unread coaching;
- a wide actionable banner with source context, primary/defer/dismiss actions, and optional follow-up chips.

Reference recording:

- [Human watch link](https://clipy.online/video/turp8hcj5pem)
- [Agent-readable context](https://clipy.online/video/turp8hcj5pem.md)

Use the recording as behavioral evidence only. Do not copy HeyClicky's name, icon, cursor artwork, exact glow/trail, colors, typography, wording, layout, animation timing, or other trade dress. The implementation must use our approved brand and original SwiftUI/AppKit presentation.

## Relationship to existing work

This issue extends #4. It does not replace #4's durable Coaching History, menu-bar unread count, Dock badge, native notification option, or custom contextual toast.

The presentation pipeline must remain:

1. detect an eligible coaching opportunity;
2. persist the event through #4's durable event/history pipeline;
3. fan out the same event ID to enabled presentation adapters;
4. record shown, suppressed, failed, and fallback outcomes;
5. leave the event recoverable from Coaching History even when every transient surface is missed.

This issue does not fix missed detection. Chrome New Tab/New Window and adjacent-tab detection remain governed by #2.

## Evidence extracted from the walkthrough

The recording is a 3:41 feature-request walkthrough. The concrete requests/patterns are:

1. **Reserved top-center presence** — around 0:24, the speaker calls out a surface in the top area that remains discoverable even when the menu bar is crowded.
2. **Compact-to-expanded coaching shelf** — around 0:38–0:47, a compact top-center prompt/status surface changes presentation while active so the current state is more obvious.
3. **Cursor opportunity halo** — around 1:08–1:21, the speaker proposes briefly lighting the cursor when an action had an available shortcut.
4. **Clear active/listening/status feedback** — around 0:38–0:47 and 1:37–2:24, visual feedback communicates that the app is listening or responding rather than silently working.
5. **Pointer-anchored popup** — around 3:23–3:25, the speaker explicitly requests an option for the coaching popup to appear where the pointer currently is instead of at a fixed screen location.
6. **Actionable decision banner** — an additional user-supplied screenshot shows a wide top-center card with two source icons, a concise title/subtitle, three prioritized decisions (decline, defer, accept), and a second row of contextual suggestion chips. This is a distinct expanded-notification form, not merely a larger toast.

Frames are evidence of the reference behavior; captions/transcript are user narration. Exact visual details must be independently designed.

## Required behavior

### 1. Presentation coordinator

Create a presentation coordinator that consumes the durable coaching event defined in #4 and routes it to independently configurable adapters.

Required properties:

- one event ID across history, menu bar, Dock, native notification, existing toast, top-center shelf, cursor halo, and pointer card;
- idempotent delivery and no duplicate cards/animations from repeated callbacks;
- deterministic policy inputs for tests: clock, active screen, pointer location, app-active state, Reduce Motion, full-screen state, cooldowns, and per-mode settings;
- structured outcomes: shown, queued, suppressed-cooldown, suppressed-quiet-hours, suppressed-disabled, suppressed-full-screen, suppressed-accessibility, blocked-permission, no-valid-placement, and presentation-failed;
- a transient presentation failure must never remove or mark the durable event seen.

### 2. Cursor opportunity halo

When a detected mouse-driven action has an eligible shortcut, optionally render a brief, noninteractive halo or pulse around the pointer.

Requirements:

- use a transparent, click-through, nonactivating overlay;
- never replace, resize, or interfere with the system pointer;
- do not block clicks, drags, contextual menus, text selection, or screen capture controls;
- show once per eligible event/batch, not continuously;
- respect quiet hours, coaching disabled state, cooldowns, and Reduce Motion;
- provide a low-motion treatment such as a static ring/fade;
- color cannot be the only indication;
- immediately tear down on screen/Space changes, display removal, app quit, or invalid geometry;
- record whether the cue was shown, suppressed, or failed.

### 3. Pointer-anchored coaching card

Add a presentation option that places the shortcut coaching card near the pointer location captured at event time.

Requirements:

- anchor to the event-time pointer position; do not chase the live pointer after presentation begins;
- prefer placement that does not cover the clicked control or pointer hot spot;
- flip horizontally/vertically near screen edges;
- clamp to the visible frame of the correct display;
- avoid the menu bar, Dock, notch/safe areas, and excluded system overlays where detectable;
- support multiple monitors with mixed scale factors and monitors positioned above/left of the primary display;
- remain nonactivating and click-through unless the user explicitly moves into an interactive card action;
- fall back to the existing contextual toast or top-center shelf when no safe placement exists;
- use a bounded duration and the same suppression/rate-limit policy as other coaching surfaces.

### 4. Top-center coaching shelf

Add an optional compact surface at the top center of the active display.

States:

- hidden/idle;
- compact unread or coaching-available indicator;
- expanded coaching card;
- evaluating/loading;
- success;
- permission/error;
- paused/quiet-hours.

Requirements:

- compact and expanded states share one coherent transition model;
- state is conveyed with icon, text, and/or shape, not color alone;
- unread coaching and error/permission state must be visually distinct;
- the shelf must not impersonate Apple's Dynamic Island, Control Center, system notifications, or hardware-notch UI;
- account for notched and non-notched Macs, full-screen apps, multiple displays, menu-bar auto-hide, and crowded menu extras;
- never cover active menu titles or system status items;
- if safe placement is unavailable, suppress with a recorded reason and rely on #4's menu/Dock/history surfaces;
- clicking an interactive shelf opens the exact coaching history item without creating duplicate windows;
- the shelf must remain an original branded component, not a pixel copy of HeyClicky.

### 5. State and transition feedback

Define a small presentation state machine so the user can tell whether the app is:

- idle;
- detecting/evaluating;
- presenting a shortcut;
- successfully recorded;
- paused by policy;
- blocked by Accessibility/notification permission;
- failed to present.

Requirements:

- no indefinite spinner or animation;
- every transient state has a timeout and deterministic fallback;
- repeated events coalesce into a batch rather than restarting animations continuously;
- errors are recoverable and link to the relevant remediation/history state;
- VoiceOver announces important state changes without narrating every animation;
- Reduce Transparency and Increase Contrast remain legible.

### 6. Settings and preview

Extend #4's Notification & Attention settings with:

- Cursor opportunity halo
- Pointer-anchored coaching card
- Top-center coaching shelf
- Animation intensity / Reduce Motion-aware behavior
- Preview each presentation mode
- Reset presentation defaults

Preview/test actions must create clearly labeled local test events, exercise the real adapter path, avoid fake analytics, and cleanly report each channel outcome.

Recommended initial policy pending owner approval:

- existing contextual toast plus durable history remain the baseline;
- cursor halo is brief and rate-limited;
- pointer-anchored card is opt-in until placement QA passes;
- top-center shelf is opt-in until notch/full-screen/menu-bar collision QA passes;
- native notifications, Dock, menu bar, and history remain independent fallbacks.

### 7. Actionable decision banner

Add an optional expanded coaching banner for events that genuinely benefit from a user decision.

The reference screenshot's useful information architecture is:

- our app icon plus the foreground/source application icon;
- one concise title and one supporting line;
- one visually dominant primary action;
- explicit defer and decline/dismiss actions;
- an optional second row of contextual follow-up chips.

Translate that pattern into shortcut coaching with original copy and styling. Appropriate actions may include **Practice Shortcut**, **Got It**, **Not Now**, **Stop Suggesting This Shortcut**, **Open in History**, or another action already supported by the durable coaching model. Do not copy the screenshot's GitHub-specific text, button labels, icon pairing, colors, glow, dimensions, or layout.

Requirements:

- reserve this larger form for decision-worthy coaching; routine events continue to use the compact toast/shelf;
- show at most one primary action, two secondary decisions, and a bounded number of follow-up chips;
- action semantics are typed and testable rather than arbitrary closures;
- defer preserves the event as unread and records the selected defer policy;
- dismiss/stop-suggesting updates the exact shortcut rule and remains reversible from Coaching History;
- opening or practicing deep-links to the exact event/shortcut without duplicate windows;
- source icons use owned app assets plus the installed application's legitimate icon where permitted, with a generic fallback and no copied third-party artwork;
- long app/action names truncate accessibly and expose their full value to VoiceOver;
- keyboard focus order, Escape, Return, Tab, and arrow navigation are deterministic;
- no accidental activation when the banner appears under the pointer;
- the banner never steals focus from the user's foreground app merely to animate;
- chips wrap, scroll, collapse, or move into an overflow menu without clipping;
- destructive or permanent suppression actions require clear wording and an undo path;
- all choices write back to #4's durable event and suppression model;
- if the banner cannot be placed safely, fall back to the compact shelf/toast and history.

## Reliability and interruption policy

- Coalesce events produced within a short burst.
- Never stack multiple pointer cards or shelves.
- Enforce per-shortcut and global cooldowns.
- Respect quiet hours and coaching-disabled state.
- Do not animate while the screen is locked or the user session is inactive.
- Avoid cursor effects during drag operations, precision drawing, games, presentations, remote-control sessions, and full-screen video where detectable; otherwise provide an exclusion setting.
- Do not capture or persist pointer trails, screen content, typed text, or private UI.
- All effects must be local-only and require no HeyClicky service, account, identifier, updater, asset, or dependency.

## Automated and installed-app QA

Add deterministic tests for:

- one event fans out once to each enabled adapter;
- duplicate callbacks do not duplicate overlays;
- suppression/failure never loses durable history;
- halo cooldown, batching, Reduce Motion, and teardown;
- pointer placement in every corner and edge of each display;
- mixed-DPI multi-monitor coordinates, negative screen origins, display disconnect, Space changes, and active-screen changes;
- notched/non-notched placement and menu-bar auto-hide;
- full-screen and presentation exclusions;
- compact → expanded → resolved/failed → hidden transitions;
- coalescing multiple events without animation restart loops;
- VoiceOver labels/announcements, Increase Contrast, Reduce Transparency, and Reduce Motion;
- app inactive/active transitions;
- relaunch/crash recovery with no stale overlay window;
- fallback to existing toast/menu/Dock/history when placement fails;
- actionable-banner primary/defer/dismiss semantics and durable state changes;
- keyboard-only operation, VoiceOver reading order, long localized copy, chip overflow, and accidental-click prevention;
- original brand asset/copy checks and absence of HeyClicky dependencies or identifiers.

Installed-app evidence must exercise the built signed app, not just unit-test view models. Record exact display geometry, mode, event ID, channel outcome, and whether the durable event remained recoverable.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] The six extracted interaction ideas are represented by original native presentation modes or an explicit owner-approved rejection.
- [ ] Cursor halo is click-through, bounded, rate-limited, accessible, and never modifies the system pointer.
- [ ] Pointer card anchors safely to event-time pointer location and falls back deterministically.
- [ ] Top-center shelf has compact/expanded/status states without colliding with menu/system UI.
- [ ] Actionable banner presents clear source context, one primary decision, defer/dismiss controls, and accessible follow-up actions without stealing focus.
- [ ] Every surface uses #4's durable event and shared event ID.
- [ ] Missed, suppressed, and failed transient presentations remain recoverable in Coaching History.
- [ ] Multi-display, notch, full-screen, Space, accessibility, and relaunch cases pass in the installed app.
- [ ] No HeyClicky branding, assets, exact visual styling, services, accounts, identifiers, updater, or runtime dependencies are present.
- [ ] Existing shortcut overlay, detection, analytics, settings, Accessibility, login item, and quit workflows regress green.
- [ ] #2 proves detection separately; this issue does not claim presentation improvements fixed event detection.

## Owner decisions before implementation

- Approve the original visual direction and brand colors/assets from #1.
- Choose default-on versus opt-in behavior for the pointer card and top-center shelf.
- Approve animation intensity, durations, cooldowns, and exclusions.
- Decide whether the top-center shelf is allowed in full-screen apps.
- Approve the exact relationship between viewing a transient surface and marking an event seen.
- Approve which coaching situations qualify for the larger actionable banner and the final action vocabulary.

## Non-goals

- Copying HeyClicky's product identity or exact UI.
- Rebuilding a voice assistant, dictation system, cursor customization product, or screen-understanding agent.
- Using presentation changes to claim that undetected actions are now reliable.
- Shipping Goal 2 UI before Goal 1 parity and #2's installed behavior are proven.

## Outcome

Make shortcut coaching reliably recoverable even when a transient toast is missed. Every detected coaching opportunity must enter a durable local history first, then independently fan out to the configured presentation surfaces:

```text
detected coaching opportunity
  → durable local history (source of truth)
  → custom contextual toast
  → menu-bar icon state + unread count
  → Dock badge + bounded attention bounce
  → optional native macOS notification
  → optional sound
```

This feature does **not** replace the event-detection and suppression diagnosis in #2. If the app never detects an action, no delivery surface can recover it. #2 remains the reliability gate for Chrome New Tab/New Window and tab-strip actions.

## Gitify interaction reference

The user supplied screenshots and authorized Gitify as an interaction reference. Local Gitify 7.3.3 and its current MIT-licensed official source were inspected.

Useful observed patterns:

- persistent menu-bar icon;
- unread count beside the icon;
- green active icon when unread items exist;
- persistent grouped notification inbox;
- mark read/done and filtering actions;
- independent settings for native system notifications, sound, notification volume, unread count, and active-icon highlighting.

Primary-source references:

- [Gitify repository](https://github.com/gitify-app/gitify)
- [Tray icon state/count handler](https://github.com/gitify-app/gitify/blob/main/src/main/handlers/tray.ts)
- [Tray settings](https://github.com/gitify-app/gitify/blob/main/src/renderer/components/settings/TraySettings.tsx)
- [System notification/sound settings](https://github.com/gitify-app/gitify/blob/main/src/renderer/components/settings/SystemSettings.tsx)
- [New-notification side effects](https://github.com/gitify-app/gitify/blob/main/src/renderer/hooks/useNotifications.ts)

Use these interaction concepts, not Gitify's logo, artwork, exact component styling, copy, or trade dress. Build an original native SwiftUI/AppKit interface consistent with the candidate's approved brand and `docs/app-replica/brand-swap-manifest.json`.

Gitify does not appear to implement a persistent Dock icon, Dock badge, or Dock bounce. Those are additional native macOS surfaces required by this issue.

## Required behavior

### 1. Durable coaching history

Create one local event for every detected eligible coaching opportunity before attempting presentation.

Each event needs:

- stable UUID;
- timestamp;
- foreground application name and bundle identifier;
- action/menu title and menu path;
- normalized shortcut and display glyphs;
- event source (`menu-bar`, `tab-strip`, `toolbar`, `context-menu`, or another characterized source);
- unread/seen/dismissed/learned state;
- per-surface delivery outcome;
- structured suppression/failure reason;
- schema version.

Store locally only. Add bounded retention (recommended: last 500 events or 30 days), clear-history control, and migration tests. Never store page content, keystrokes, private menu contents unrelated to the selected action, or secrets.

The history is the source of truth. A toast or system-banner failure must never discard the event.

### 2. Always-visible menu-bar surface

Keep an original menu-bar icon present whenever the app is running.

State rules:

- zero unread events → neutral monochrome template icon, no number;
- one or more unread events → approved green active icon plus unread count;
- offline/permission/error state → a distinct non-green status treatment so “new coaching” cannot be confused with failure;
- loading/refresh state must not erase the unread count.

Clicking the icon opens a compact native popover showing the most recent items, unread count, **Mark All Seen**, and **Open Coaching History…**. The popover may use Gitify's information hierarchy as a reference but must have an original native layout, terminology, icons, spacing, and visual system.

### 3. Persistent Coaching History UI

Add **Coaching History** to the planned Home/management UI in `docs/settings-ui-plan.md`.

Minimum capabilities:

- newest-first list;
- filter by unread, application, and shortcut;
- search action/shortcut text;
- show why a transient surface was suppressed or failed;
- mark seen/unseen;
- dismiss a shortcut permanently;
- restore dismissed shortcuts;
- clear history with confirmation;
- empty, loading, migration-error, and permission-paused states;
- keyboard navigation and VoiceOver labels.

Opening the history marks items seen only according to an explicit rule. Recommended: opening does not clear everything; items become seen when displayed or through **Mark All Seen**. Specify and test the final rule.

### 4. Existing contextual toast

Retain the custom nonactivating coaching toast as the immediate, in-context channel.

Improve it so every attempt records one of:

- `shown`;
- `suppressed-cooldown`;
- `suppressed-hourly-cap`;
- `suppressed-quiet-hours`;
- `suppressed-disabled`;
- `suppressed-excluded-app`;
- `suppressed-dismissed-shortcut`;
- `blocked-permission`;
- `presentation-failed`.

A suppressed toast still leaves an unread history event unless the final product policy explicitly says that suppression also marks it seen. Do not silently drop it.

### 5. Native macOS notifications

Add optional `UNUserNotificationCenter` delivery for new coaching events.

Requirements:

- explain the benefit before requesting authorization;
- keep native notifications independently configurable;
- accurately show denied, provisional/quiet, banner-disabled, and authorized states where discoverable;
- clicking a notification opens the exact history item;
- deduplicate against the same event ID;
- never classify Focus/scheduled delivery as app-level delivery success without distinguishing “submitted to system” from “banner visibly observed”;
- do not use Time Sensitive or Critical interruption levels for routine coaching;
- custom toast, history, menu count, and Dock badge remain functional when system notifications are denied or suppressed.

Recommended default: history/menu state/custom toast enabled; native notification and sound presented as explicit user choices during onboarding/settings.

### 6. Always-visible Dock icon and badge

Change the candidate from an accessory-only `LSUIElement` identity to a regular activation policy so its app icon remains in the Dock while running. Preserve the menu-bar icon as an additional surface.

Requirements:

- original owned app icon;
- red system-defined Dock badge containing the same unread count as history/menu state;
- counts use one shared source of truth and update atomically;
- badge clears/decrements only under the approved seen/dismissed rule;
- fresh launch, relaunch, quit, login-item launch, multiple Spaces, and full-screen behavior tested;
- clicking the Dock icon opens or focuses Coaching History/Home rather than an empty process;
- no duplicate Home windows.

Use `NSApp.dockTile.badgeLabel` for the system-defined badge appearance. The current `LSUIElement=true` behavior and Goal 1 menu-bar-only acceptance become an explicit owner-approved product difference; update manifests/evidence rather than pretending both identities pass simultaneously.

### 7. Dock attention bounce

Use only an informational attention request, never a critical indefinite bounce, for ordinary coaching.

Recommended policy:

- at most one one-second informational bounce for a newly arrived batch;
- only while the app is inactive;
- maximum once per five minutes;
- cancel the outstanding request when Home/History activates or the batch is marked seen;
- user setting: **Bounce Dock icon for new coaching**;
- respect quiet hours and coaching-disabled state;
- expose the rate-limit/suppression reason to tests.

Do not bounce for every shortcut opportunity. Repeated bouncing would turn a reliability feature into interruption spam.

### 8. Settings

Add a **Notification & Attention** section with independent controls:

- Contextual coaching toast
- Native macOS notifications
- Sound and volume
- Menu-bar unread count
- Green menu-bar highlight
- Dock unread badge
- Dock bounce
- Quiet hours
- Retention duration / maximum history items
- Clear history
- Open macOS Notification Settings when authorization needs attention

Include a **Send Test Coaching Event** action that creates a clearly labeled local test event and exercises each enabled surface without recording fake usage analytics.

## Reliability and observability requirements

- One event ID across history, toast, menu bar, Dock, and native notification.
- Idempotent delivery: retries cannot create duplicate history rows or duplicate badges.
- Persist unread state before presenting transient surfaces.
- Restore correct counts after crash/relaunch.
- Surface channel-specific outcomes in debug/test evidence without logging private content.
- Provide a deterministic clock and in-memory history store for tests.
- Keep event detection, policy, durable storage, and presentation adapters behind separate interfaces.

## QA additions to #2

Add test cases for:

- event persists when every transient channel is unavailable;
- menu/Dock/history counts remain identical across add/seen/dismiss/clear/relaunch;
- green menu icon appears only for unread coaching;
- permission/error icon states cannot be mistaken for unread state;
- native notifications denied, quiet, Focus-delayed, and authorized;
- notification click deep-links to the exact history item;
- informational bounce rate limiting and cancellation;
- no bounce while active, during quiet hours, or when disabled;
- Dock badge with 0, 1, 2, 99, and large counts;
- history retention/migration/corruption recovery;
- no duplicate event under repeated callbacks/retries;
- Reduce Motion/accessibility behavior and VoiceOver labels;
- menu-bar-only-to-regular-app activation migration and login-item behavior.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Every detected coaching opportunity is recoverable from local history even if no transient alert is seen.
- [ ] Menu bar is always present and shows the exact unread count.
- [ ] Menu icon turns approved green for unread state and has distinct error/offline/permission states.
- [ ] Coaching History supports review, filtering, seen state, dismissal, and clearing.
- [ ] Custom toast remains immediate and reports structured delivery/suppression outcomes.
- [ ] Native notifications are optional, permission-aware, deduplicated, and deep-link correctly.
- [ ] Dock icon is always present, opens Home/History, and uses the system red badge with the shared unread count.
- [ ] Dock performs one bounded informational bounce according to the declared policy.
- [ ] All surfaces survive relaunch and converge on one event/count state.
- [ ] Existing shortcut overlay, detection, analytics, settings, Accessibility, login item, and quit workflows regress green.
- [ ] #2's installed E2E harness proves channel outcomes independently; unit tests alone do not establish visible delivery.
- [ ] Original brand assets and native UI replace Gitify-specific artwork/copy/layout.

## Explicit non-goal

Adding more presentation surfaces does not fix missed event detection. Chrome New Tab/New Window and adjacent-tab detection remain governed by #2 and their own future defect/enhancement tickets.

comments.body:
## Multi-surface reliability expansion

Issue #4 now specifies the product-side delivery architecture: durable coaching history as the source of truth, custom contextual toast, always-visible menu-bar unread state/count, optional native notifications and sound, and an always-visible Dock icon with a shared red badge and bounded informational bounce.

Add these cases to the living QA matrix during the next executable pass:

- history persists even when every transient channel is unavailable;
- menu-bar, Dock, and history counts remain identical through add/seen/dismiss/clear/relaunch;
- green menu icon only represents unread coaching;
- offline/error/permission states are visually distinct;
- native notification denied/quiet/Focus-delayed/authorized paths;
- notification click deep-links to the exact history item;
- one-second informational Dock bounce rate limit/cancellation;
- no bounce while active, disabled, or in quiet hours;
- Dock badge boundaries: 0, 1, 2, 99, and large counts;
- history retention, migration, corruption recovery, and deduplication;
- accessory-to-regular activation migration, login-item launch, and no duplicate Home windows.

More surfaces do not fix missed event detection. The Chrome New Tab/New Window and adjacent-tab cases remain the P0 detection gate in this issue.

## Brand-asset follow-up from #4

The approved product identity now also needs an original stateful icon family:

- neutral monochrome menu-bar template icon;
- approved green unread variant;
- distinct offline/error/permission variants that cannot be confused with unread state;
- original full-color Dock/app icon suitable for the system red badge;
- light/dark/high-contrast and Retina variants;
- documented semantic color tokens and provenance/assignment for every asset.

Gitify is an interaction reference only. Do not copy its `G` artwork, exact icon shapes, component styling, copy, or layout. The functional pattern—neutral idle, green unread, visible count—may be independently implemented with original candidate branding.

Issue #4 is blocked on these owner-approved brand assets and decisions.
